/T (2.1 Social Media and Academic Performance: An Overview )
/E (2.1 Social Media and Academic Performance: An Overview )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The academic study of social media's impact on student performance emerged rapidly in the late 2000s, driven by the explosive growth of Facebook as a campus phenomenon. Early research by Kirschner and Karpinski \(2010\) established a foundational finding: Facebook users reported lower GPAs and spent fewer hours per week studying than non-users, even after controlling for other variables. This initial contribution sparked a proliferation of studies across diverse national contexts, with broadly convergent results indicating a negative association between social media engagement and academic performance, though with considerable variation in effect sizes and methodological approaches. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
