174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 99 0 R
/E (Andreassen et al. \(2016\), in a large-scale national Norwegian survey, found significant positive associations between addictive social media use and academic procrastination, mediated partly through narcissistic personality traits and poor self-esteem. More recently, Kim \(2020\) demonstrated in a longitudinal study that procrastination fully mediated the relationship between social media use and academic performance decline, confirming its role as a critical pathway rather than a mere correlate. The current study contributes to this body of evidence by examining procrastination as both an individual predictor and a component of a broader composite distraction measure within an Indian student sample.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
