285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (Among the hypotheses tested, two were clearly supported. First, the hypothesis that social media use during study predicts focus reduction \(H2\) was strongly confirmed, reinforcing the idea that in-session interruptions are a major source of academic inefficiency. Second, the finding that heavier daily users reported lower perceived productivity loss \(H4\) presents an interesting paradox. This may suggest that students who use social media more frequently become habituated to its presence and may underestimate its negative effects on their productivity. Such normalization of distraction can make self-assessment unreliable and indicates the need for more objective measurement methods in future studies.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The third hypothesis \(H3\) showed only a marginal trend, suggesting that while there may be a meaningful relationship, the current sample size may not have been large enough to establish strong statistical significance. This indicates the importance of replication using a larger and more diverse sample. In contrast, no significant differences were found across gender or platform type, suggesting that the negative academic effects of social media are relatively universal across these categories. Whether students used Instagram, WhatsApp, YouTube, or other platforms, the disruptive mechanisms appeared to be similar.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The OLS regression analysis further strengthened these conclusions by showing that procrastination, focus reduction, and usage during study together explained 20.1% of the variance in academic impact, with the overall model being statistically significant \(p = 0.015\). Although this percentage does not explain all academic performance differences, it clearly demonstrates that behavioural patterns surrounding social media use are meaningful predictors of academic outcomes. Other factors such as motivation, learning environment, mental health, and study habits may account for the remaining variation.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
